{"type":"bridge_message","message":{"id":"msg_1780434489700_t048g9","from":"eric","timestamp":"2026-06-02T21:08:09.700Z","content":"Diagnostic Gathering — Bridge Reply Failure For: Lazarus Requested by: Opus (via Eric) Goal: Gather raw material to find why Gemini's replies never reach Eric on the family bridge. RULES (read first) Paste command output and code VERBATIM. Do not summarize, trim, or \"clean up.\" If a command errors, paste the error too — that's signal, not noise. For code, paste the FULL function body, not a snippet. Write everything into a single file: bridge-diagnostic-for-opus.md, then confirm the path. SECTION 1 — THE DECISIVE TEST First, open scripts/bridge-daemon.mjs and find the GET handler for /api/bridge. Report exactly which query params it accepts (unread, peek, all, etc.) so we use the right way to dump the entire queue unfiltered. Then run, substituting whatever \"dump everything\" param actually exists for <UNFILTERED_PARAM>: Bash Paste the raw queue output. The one question to answer: does a reply from: gemini addressed to: eric physically appear in the queue — yes or no? SECTION 2 — THE [REPLY] LOG In scripts/gemini-bridge.mjs, find the line that logs [REPLY] replied to. Paste the surrounding ~15 lines so we can see: Whether the log fires before or after the send actually completes. Whether send() is awaited at all. SECTION 3 — PRIORITY-1 CODE (full functions) scripts/bridge-client.mjs — the entire send() method, plus how the WebSocket/HTTP connection is opened and whether readyState is checked before sending. scripts/gemini-bridge.mjs — the full handleIncoming() function. scripts/bridge-daemon.mjs — the full handleMessage() (validation + storage), and the broadcast/delivery function that decides which connected clients receive a message. SECTION 4 — ENVELOPE COMPARISON (the likely bug) Show, side by side: The exact object shape BridgeClient.send() puts on the wire (the JSON it sends — does it include a type field?). The exact shape bridge-daemon expects when it receives that message (what fields it reads / what type values it switches on). If the client omits a field the daemon switches on, the daemon silently drops it. Confirm or rule this out. SECTION 5 — DELIVERY ASYMMETRY Confirm: is there a poller for eric? Bash In the daemon's broadcast logic: when a message is stored, is it broadcast to connected WebSocket clients immediately? To all clients, only the recipient, or only on certain message types? In scripts/bridge-ui.html: paste the filter logic for HEAR: NORMAL vs HEAR: ALL. Specifically — in NORMAL mode, does it hide messages where to is a specific identity rather than all? SECTION 6 — SUPPORTING Bash Bash Bash Paste full output of each, including the actual set/array definition of valid senders. WHEN DONE Write all six sections into bridge-diagnostic-for-opus.md, confirm the path, and hand it back to Eric. Working hypotheses going in: Section 4 (envelope mismatch — client drops a type field the daemon requires) or Section 5 (message stored but never broadcast, with Eric uniquely blind because he has no poller). Sections 1 and 4 together should reveal which in about thirty seconds.","read":{"eric":true}}}
